  "meta": {
    "title": "Palindromize: w ↦ wwᴿ",
    "blurb": "Writes the word, then writes it again backwards, so every output is an even palindrome. One pass right, one pass left, printing as it goes — a function no one-way finite transducer can compute.",
    "inputs": [
      {
        "w": "ab",
        "expect": "accept",
        "out": "abba"
      },
      {
        "w": "aab",
        "expect": "accept",
        "out": "aabbaa"
      },
      {
        "w": "b",
        "expect": "accept",
        "out": "bb"
      },
      {
        "w": "abab",
        "expect": "accept",
        "out": "ababbaba"
      },
      {
        "w": "",
        "expect": "accept",
        "out": "",
        "label": "ε"
      }
    ],
    "library": {
      "author": {
        "login": "thethinkmachine"
      },
      "license": "CC-BY-4.0",
      "tags": [
        "two-way",
        "palindromes",
        "reversal"
      ],
      "difficulty": "intro",
      "readme": "A one-way transducer reads each symbol once and must write what it is going to write about it then, with finite memory — so it cannot produce wᴿ at the end of an unbounded w. A two-way head simply goes back for it.\n\nTwo-way deterministic transducers compute exactly the *regular functions*, a class closed under composition, and strictly larger than what one-way transducers compute: reversal, duplication (**Copy twice: w ↦ ww**) and this one are all in it."
    }
  }
}
